// `avg` OVER A `bool` COLUMN — true is 1.0, false is 0.0.
//
// THE RULING (Victor, option (c) of three): `avg` ADMITS `bool` as numeric with
// the value rule true→1.0 / false→0.0, and REFUSES every other non-numeric
// argument. The other two options were to refuse `bool` as well (which breaks
// programs that compile and answer today) and to leave the hole open (which
// lets `avg(str)` keep compiling).
//
// ⚠ THE RULE IS NOT INVENTED HERE — IT IS WHAT `as f64` ALREADY MEANS. mlir-gen
// zero-extends i1 before the conversion on purpose (`sitofp(i1(1))` = -1.0 is
// wrong, `uitofp(i1(1))` = 1.0 is right). The ruling makes the FRONT END admit it.
//
// ⚠ AND THE CLAUSE THAT CARRIES IT IS NOT THE ONE THE SENTENCE ABOVE SUGGESTS.
// The int→float arm of `MLIRGenImpl::gen_expr_kind(ECastView, …)` decides
// `src_unsigned` from TWO disjuncts: an i1 TYPE test on the MLIR value, and
// `LogosType::is_unsigned_repr_kind(op_ty)`, which names `Bool` explicitly
// (`include/logos/compiler/sema.hpp`).
//
// ⚠ RE-MEASURED 2026-08-10, AND THE EARLIER READING OF IT WAS WRONG. This header
// used to say "only the second is load-bearing" and cite a 13 for excluding
// `Kind::Bool` from it. NEITHER disjunct is uniquely load-bearing: they are
// MUTUALLY REDUNDANT, and each ALONE is sufficient. Four builds, each predicted
// before it was run, each reverted:
//   · drop the i1 type test only ............................. GREEN (exit 0)
//   · exclude `Kind::Bool` at THIS SITE only, i1 test kept ... GREEN (exit 0)
//   · drop `k == Kind::Bool` from `is_unsigned_repr_kind`
//     itself in `include/logos/compiler/sema.hpp`, i1 kept ... GREEN (exit 0)
//   · drop the i1 test AND exclude `Bool` ..................... RED, exit 13
// So the value rule IS pinned by this file, but only against the removal of
// BOTH clauses; no single-clause edit here can red it. The earlier "13" is
// reproducible only as the LAST line above — i.e. as a control applied on top of
// the previous one rather than after restoring it.
// Corollary, measured separately: with the i1 test deleted the ENTIRE L2 sample
// is green (1892/1892 + 12 684 generated cases + 31 gates), so that disjunct is
// dead across the corpus, not merely here. Deleting it is NOT licensed by this
// file — the `Bool` clause in `is_unsigned_repr_kind` would then be the only
// thing standing between `sitofp(i1 1)` and -1.0, which is exit 13 above.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE WAS `wql_agg_avg_bool_three_engines` AND IS RENAMED, NOT MERELY
// EDITED, BECAUSE THE OLD NAME WOULD HAVE BECOME A FALSE STATEMENT. P5 deleted
// the dynamic `Query::compile` executor and the incremental `Query::incremental`
// engine; only the static `deem!` emitter is left. The three-engine claim was the
// point of the old file — admitting bool in the one shared table (`el_quot_ty`)
// moved all three at once — and IT IS LOST, not preserved under a new spelling.
// What remains is the VALUE RULE on the surviving engine. The shared table still
// has a refusal sensor of its own: `tests/logos/fail/wql_agg_uda_f32_arg_fail.logos`.
//
// ⚠ THE EXPECTATIONS ARE WRITTEN-OUT CONSTANTS, DERIVED BY HAND, NOT READ OFF A
// RUN — the discipline `query_minmax_float_seed_leak` states: an oracle that
// computes the answer by folding just reproduces whichever fold it was written
// with. By hand, over the group's rows in first-occurrence order:
//   g=0  { true, true, true, false }  → (1.0+1.0+1.0+0.0)/4 = 3.0/4 = 0.75
//   g=1  { true }                     → 1.0/1               = 1.0
//   g=2  { false }                    → 0.0/1               = 0.0
// All three are exact in binary f64 (3/4 = 0.11₂), so `==` is the right compare
// and no tolerance is involved.
//
// ⚠ 0.0 GETS ITS OWN EXIT CODE. A fix that admits bool in the table but not in
// the value read makes the engine ACCEPT the query and silently answer 0.0. That
// failure must be legible AS ITSELF and not as a generic mismatch, so it is
// asserted before the general compare.
//
// ⚠ THE SECOND ARM (`ref_avg_bool`) IS AN INDEPENDENT ORACLE, NOT A SECOND ENGINE.
// P5 removed the second implementation, so what was a three-engine differential
// became hand constants against one engine. The constants pin the ANSWER but not
// the RULE: they are three numbers, and a value read that is wrong in a way the
// three shapes happen not to separate would keep them green. `ref_avg_bool` walks
// the `Vec<S>` with a plain `while` loop and reaches NOTHING in `logos.std.wql`,
// nothing in `logos.mem.deem`, and nothing the emitter emits — its callees are
// `Vec::get`, `Vec::len` and arithmetic. Critically it does NOT spell `as f64` on
// a bool: it branches `if r.b { 1.0f64 } else { 0.0f64 }`, so the very coercion
// this file rules on is on the SUBJECT side only. Inverting that branch here does
// not move the engine, and breaking the engine's bool value read does not move
// this. It is asserted BESIDE the literals, never instead of them.
//
// ⚠ FOUR CONTROLS, EACH PREDICTED BEFORE IT WAS RUN, EACH RESTORED:
//  (A) engine-side, INVISIBLE TO THE LITERALS: in the group fold's `inner`
//      quote_block (`stdlib/mem/wql/rexpr_walk.logos`) start the `__g_key` dedup
//      scan at the LAST key instead of 0 — a contiguous-grouping regression.
//      `rows()` is group-sorted, so 11–18 stay GREEN; the interleaved corpus
//      splits into 7 groups. PREDICTED 41, MEASURED 41. This is the whole reason
//      the second corpus exists: no hand constant in this file can see it.
//  (B) fixture-side: invert `if r.b` in `ref_avg_bool`. PREDICTED 16, MEASURED 16
//      — with 12–15 green, so the two sides are one-sided, not a shared fold.
//  (C) engine-side value rule. ⚠ AS ORIGINALLY WRITTEN THIS CONTROL DID NOT
//      REPRODUCE — see the RE-MEASURED block at the top. The `Kind::Bool`
//      exclusion ALONE is GREEN; what reds at 13 is dropping the i1 test AND
//      excluding `Bool`. PREDICTED 13, MEASURED 13 for that two-clause edit —
//      the literals catch the value rule FIRST, which is the right order and
//      says the arm did NOT move with the engine.
//  (D) engine-side, INVISIBLE TO THE LITERALS **AND TO 41** — the control that
//      fires `ref_avg_bool` itself rather than `ref_group_keys`. In the same
//      `inner` quote_block, after `__g_cnt` is bumped at the row's own group,
//      redirect the accumulator folds to the NEWEST group
//      (`__gi = __g_key.len() - 1i64;` before `#( #folds; )*`). On a
//      group-SORTED corpus the row's group always IS the newest, so `rows()` is
//      answered perfectly and 11–18 stay green; the group COUNT is untouched, so
//      41 stays green too. On the interleaved corpus group 7 collects only its
//      first row: sum 1 over count 4 = 0.25 against a walked 0.75.
//      PREDICTED 42, MEASURED 42. (A) alone would NOT have shown this: (A) is
//      caught by `ref_group_keys` at the count. This one needs the WALKED VALUE.
// The catches are VALUE catches, not row-count refusals: (A) is caught at a
// walked group COUNT after the whole 8-row fold ran, (B) and (C) at a projected
// f64 after scan, group, fold and finalize.
//
// exit 0 = the emitted engine answers 0.75 / 1.0 / 0.0.
// Exit codes 20–37 named the dynamic and incremental engines; retired, not reused.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct S { pub g: i64, pub b: bool }

pub deem q_avg(ss: &[S]) { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = avg(s.b) select a : f64 }

// The 3-of-4-true shape, plus a singleton true and a singleton false so that
// true→1.0 and false→0.0 are each pinned on their own and not only through the
// quotient.
fn rows() -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, b: false });
    v.push(S { g: 1i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 2i64, b: false });
    return v;
}

// THE SECOND CORPUS — INTERLEAVED, and deliberately WITHOUT hand constants.
// `rows()` is sorted by group, so every defect it can show is a value-rule defect
// and the three literals already see those. The shapes the literals CANNOT see are
// the ones where a group's rows are not contiguous: the emitted fold finds a group
// by a linear `__g_key` scan (`rexpr_walk.logos`, the `inner` quote_block), and a
// regression that looked only at the newest key would answer `rows()` correctly
// and split every group here. This corpus exists to be read by `ref_avg_bool`
// alone. Keys 7 and 3, four rows each, both averages exact in binary f64
// (7 → t,f,t,t = 3/4; 3 → f,t,t,f = 2/4), and the two 3-rows in the middle are
// ADJACENT on purpose so a contiguity defect cannot be dismissed as "it merged
// nothing".
fn rows_ilv() -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    v.push(S { g: 7i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 3i64, b: false });
    v.push(S { g: 7i64, b: false });
    v.push(S { g: 3i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 7i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 3i64, b: true  });
    v.push(S { g: 3i64, b: false });
    v.push(S { g: 7i64, b: true  });
    return v;
}

// First-occurrence order of the group keys, walked in the fixture. This is the
// output order the emitted group-row phase produces; recomputing it here is what
// lets the arm address a group WITHOUT the engine telling it which group a column
// belongs to.
fn ref_group_keys(ss: &Vec<S>) -> Vec<i64> {
    let mut ks: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < ss.len() {
        let k: i64 = ss.get(i).g;
        let mut seen: bool = false;
        let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
        while j < ks.len() { if ks.get(j) == k { seen = true; } j = j + 1i64; }
        if !seen { ks.push(k); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return ks;
}

// The independent arm. No `as f64` on a bool anywhere in it; no wql/deem callee.
fn ref_avg_bool(ss: &Vec<S>, g: i64) -> f64 {
    let mut sum: f64 = 0.0f64;
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < ss.len() {
        let r: S = ss.get(i);
        if r.g == g {
            if r.b { sum = sum + 1.0f64; } else { sum = sum + 0.0f64; }
            n = n + 1i64;
        }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    if n == 0i64 { return 0.0f64; }
    return sum / (n as f64);
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let ss: Vec<S> = rows();

    let sv: Vec<f64> = match q_avg(ss.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(v)  => { v }
        Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    if sv.len() != 3i64                { return 11i32; }
    if sv.get(0i64) == 0.0f64          { return 12i32; }   // the 0.0 signature
    if sv.get(0i64) != 0.75f64         { return 13i32; }
    if sv.get(1i64) != 1.0f64          { return 14i32; }
    if sv.get(2i64) != 0.0f64          { return 15i32; }

    // The independent arm, group by group. 16/17/18 are ITS exit codes; a red
    // here with 13/14/15 green would mean the constants and the walk disagree,
    // which cannot happen unless the fixture itself is wrong — and a red here
    // WITH 13/14/15 red says the engine moved, not the constants.
    if sv.get(0i64) != ref_avg_bool(&ss, 0i64) { return 16i32; }
    if sv.get(1i64) != ref_avg_bool(&ss, 1i64) { return 17i32; }
    if sv.get(2i64) != ref_avg_bool(&ss, 2i64) { return 18i32; }

    // ── the interleaved corpus: the arm is the ONLY sensor here ─────────────
    // 40+ because 20–37 are retired names and must not be reused.
    let ss2: Vec<S> = rows_ilv();
    let ks2: Vec<i64> = ref_group_keys(&ss2);

    let sv2: Vec<f64> = match q_avg(ss2.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(v)  => { v }
        Result::Err(_e) => { return 40i32; }
    };
    // Not `!= 2i64`: the expected count is WALKED, so this line stays true if the
    // corpus is ever edited. A group split shows up here first.
    if sv2.len() != ks2.len() { return 41i32; }
    let mut i2: i64 = 0i64;
    while i2 < ks2.len() {
        if sv2.get(i2) != ref_avg_bool(&ss2, ks2.get(i2)) { return 42i32 + (i2 as i32); }
        i2 = i2 + 1i64;
    }

    return 0i32;
}
